WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.620
In this video, you migrate a Linux virtual machine from VMware vSphere to SUSE Virtualization with a cold migration plan in the SUSE VM Migration Toolkit add-on.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:20.570
The SUSE VM Migration Toolkit add-on adds the Virtual Machine Migration menu.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.250
Select Providers.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:31.210
The vsphere provider connects to vCenter and is Ready. The host provider is this cluster, the migration target.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.520
Expand the Virtual Machine Migration menu again,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.460
open Migration Overview, which lists the migration plans,

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.610
and select Create Migration.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:50.430
In step one, select the existing provider vsphere instead of creating a new one.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:57.430
The form fills in the vCenter URL and credentials from the provider. Select Proceed.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:04.110
Step two lists the virtual machines in vCenter. Search for ubuntu-nfs-src,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:13.350
a small, powered-off Ubuntu virtual machine with one CPU, two gigabytes of memory, and a ten gigabyte disk. Select it.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.960
Select Proceed.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:25.950
Step three maps vSphere resources to SUSE Virtualization. Map the port group VM Network to the management network,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:31.890
and the datastore ssd to the harvester-longhorn storage class.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:38.420
The mapping defaults to Filesystem volumes. Select Edit to change the volume mode.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:49.230
Choose Block, the volume mode that SUSE Virtualization virtual machine disks normally use. Keep ReadWriteMany for live migration.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.730
Select Apply Override.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.350
Select Proceed.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.770
Step four reviews the plan. Name it demo-ubuntu-plan,

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.330
and create the virtual machine in the namespace demo.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:11.350
This is a cold migration. Source virtual machines are powered off during the copy. This one is already off.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.160
Select Create and Start.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:23.040
The plan starts. Its row shows each step, from disk allocation and guest conversion to the disk copy.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:30.340
After the copy, the plan shows Succeeded, and the virtual machine exists in SUSE Virtualization.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.300
Open Virtual Machines.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:40.070
ubuntu-nfs-src is in the namespace demo. It is off, like its source in vSphere.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.890
Open its actions menu,

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.630
and select Start.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:56.790
The migrated Ubuntu virtual machine is running in SUSE Virtualization, converted by virt-v2v for KubeVirt.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:06.822
The virtual machine from vSphere now runs in SUSE Virtualization. Next, you find the largest virtual machine you can run.
